sources:
    - id: whr2026
      title: Self-reported life satisfaction vs. GDP per capita (Cantril ladder, 2025)
      authors: Our World in Data, from the World Happiness Report 2026 (Wellbeing Research Centre, Gallup World Poll) and the World Bank
      year: 2026
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/gdp-vs-happiness
      finding: 140 countries with 2025 values; life satisfaction is the average answer on a 0 (worst possible life) to 10 (best possible life) ladder; GDP per capita in constant 2021 international dollars (PPP). Finland 7.76 at $55,700; United States 6.82 at $76,900.
    - id: easterlin1974
      title: Does Economic Growth Improve the Human Lot? Some Empirical Evidence
      authors: Richard A. Easterlin
      year: 1974
      finding: At a point in time richer people within a country report more happiness; in the United States, 1946–1970, average reported happiness did not rise as incomes grew.

    text src "World Happiness Report 2026, via OWID" #8f887c cites ["whr2026"] x 830 y 165 font_size 20 hidden true
    text ladder10 "10 = best\npossible life" #5e8c4f x 830 y 520 font_size 34 hidden true
    text ladder0 "0 = worst\npossible life" #b5482e x 830 y 330 font_size 34 hidden true
    text steep_note "steep" #b5482e at.data [22,3] font_size 30 hidden true
    text flat_note "flat?" #2f6b8f at.data [118,7.75] font_size 30 hidden true
    text eas_t "Easterlin, 1974" #3d3833 cites ["easterlin1974"] x 830 y 610 font_size 32 hidden true
    text par1 "At a moment:\nricher → happier" #5e8c4f cites ["easterlin1974"] x 830 y 510 font_size 32 hidden true
    text par2 "Over time:\ngrowth → no gain" #b5482e cites ["easterlin1974"] x 830 y 330 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "Richer, happier?"

Is happiness what we maximise? And if it is, does more money buy more of it?
    draw axes

B: Start with how anyone measures it. Gallup asks people in ab (@draw ladder0@)out a hundred and forty countries to picture a ladder: zero is the  (@draw ladder10@)worst possible life for you, ten the best.

Which step are you on? Average the answers and each country gets a score: its life satisfaction.
    highlight axes part "Life satisfaction (0–10)"

    erase ladder0 ladder10

Now set it against income: GDP per person, in dollars adjusted for what things cost locally.
    highlight axes part "GDP per person ($ thousand)"

B: My guess: it levels off. (@draw flat_note@) Once you're comfortable, money stops buying happiness.

    quiz "Among the richest countries, does more income still go with higher life satisfaction?" judge false
        right: Hold that guess: here come the data.
        * Yes, it keeps rising
        * It levels off
        * It falls

    erase flat_note

Here are the countries, in 2025.
    draw points src

Among the poorest the scores climb steeply. Among the (@draw flat_note@) rich, the cloud looks nearly flat.
    draw steep_note

B: So I was right.
    highlight flat_note effect circle

    erase steep_note flat_note

That's close to what Richard Easterlin argued in 1974. Within a cou (@draw par1@)ntry, richer people do say they are happier.
    draw eas_t

But as America grew richer from  (@draw par2@)1946 to 1970, its average happiness did not rise.

That's the Easterlin paradox: richer beats poorer at any moment, yet growth doesn't seem to lift a nation's happiness over time.
    highlight par1 par2 effect box

    pause 0.5

