    text tk6 "$64k" #8f887c at.data [6,2.3] font_size 20 hidden true
    text tk7 "$128k" #8f887c at.data [7,2.3] font_size 20 hidden true
    text k1 "+$1,000:\nMalawi: a lot\nNorway: a crumb" #3d3833 x 830 y 450 font_size 30 hidden true
    text src "World Happiness Report 2026, via OWID" #8f887c cites ["whr2026"] x 830 y 165 font_size 20 hidden true
    text slope "+0.5 step\nper doubling" #8a5fa8 cites ["whr2026"] x 830 y 560 font_size 36 hidden true
    text sw "Stevenson & Wolfers,\n2008: no flat top" #3d3833 cites ["sw2008"] x 830 y 400 font_size 28 hidden true
    text util "U ≈ log I" #8a5fa8 x 830 y 490 font_size 44 hidden true
    text util_t "each dollar\nadds less" #3d3833 x 830 y 380 font_size 30 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch mcard work [{"tex":"\\text{ladder} \\approx -1.69 + 1.74\\,\\log_{10} I"},{"tex":"+\\$100 \\text{ at } \\$2\\text{k}: +0.037"},{"tex":"+\\$100 \\text{ at } \\$64\\text{k}: +0.0012"}] x 810 y 400 font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "A fairer ruler"

B: The dollar scale is the wrong ruler. A thousand do (@draw k1@)llars more is a lot in Malawi and next to nothing in Norway.
    draw axes

B: So count income in doublings inste (@draw tk0 tk1 tk2 tk3@)ad: one thousand, two, four, eight, up to a  (@draw tk4 tk5 tk6 tk7@)hundred and twenty-eight thousand.

    erase k1

Same countries, same scores. And the flat top is gone: it's a straight, rising band.
    draw points src

B: Each doubling of income goes with about half a step up the ladder, from the poorest country to the richest.
    draw fit_line slope

That's what Betsey Stevenson and Justin Wolfers showed in 2008: no point where it flat (@highlight fit_line@)tens, and much the same slope as between richer and poorer people inside one country.
    draw sw

B: So I wasn't right after all: the flat top was the dollar scale talking. Forty to eighty thousand takes forty thousand dollars for the same half step that a thousand buys at the bottom.
    highlight tk5 tk6 effect underline

    erase slope sw

B: Your turn. The line is what income predicts; some countries sit far from it.
    draw point_121 point_135 point_86 point_69

    ask "Of the four named countries, which sits furthest above the line? Tap it." choose ["point_121","point_135","point_86","point_69"] answer point_86
        right: Costa Rica: well above the line for its income.
        wrong: The highest dot isn't the one furthest above the line: compare each with the line right under it. Here, Costa Rica.

Costa Rica: just over a third of America's income, and more than a full step above its line. Botswana sits more than two steps below.
    highlight point_86 point_69

B: And this shape is an old friend. A log curve like this is one conv (@draw util util_t@)enient way economists draw a ranking over income: each extra dollar adds less than the one before.

    pause 0.3

    hide util util_t

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own store m default "Skip it"

@math

    erase mathq

    hide util util_t

Fitted across all hundred and forty countries, the ladder score is about minus one point seven, plus one point seven four times the base-ten log of income.
    draw mcard_box mcard_line_1

A log's slope falls as one over income. So an extra hundred dollars adds about four hundredths of a step where income is two thousand dollars.
    draw mcard_line_2

At sixty-four thousand, the same hundred dollars adds about a thousandth: thirty-two times less.
    draw mcard_line_3

    erase mcard

@test

    erase mathq

    show util util_t

    pause 0.3

## Satisfied, or happy?
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - $15k
      - $30k
      - $60k
      - $120k
      - $240k
    series:
      - {name: Judging life, values: [3.6, 4.6, 5.6, 6.6, 7.6], color: '#3d3833'}
      - {name: 'Feelings, 2010', values: [2.4, 3.3, 4.1, 4.4, 4.4], color: '#b5482e'}
      - {name: 'Feelings, 2021', values: [2.4, 3.2, 4, 4.8, 5.6], color: '#2f6b8f'}
      - {name: Least happy fifth, values: [0.8, 1.6, 2.4, 2.9, 2.9], color: '#b5482e'}
      - {name: Middle, values: [3, 3.8, 4.6, 5.4, 6.2], color: '#2f6b8f'}
      - {name: Happiest, values: [5, 5.7, 6.4, 7.6, 9], color: '#5e8c4f'}
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 10
    y_ticks: false
    x_label: Household income
    y_label: Better (schematic)
    label_size: 22
    box:
      x: 80
      'y': 140
      w: 520
      h: 470
sources:
    - id: kd2010
      title: High income improves evaluation of life but not emotional well-being
      authors: Daniel Kahneman and Angus Deaton
      year: 2010
      doi: 10.1073/pnas.1011492107
      finding: 'PNAS 107(38). Over 450,000 responses to the Gallup-Healthways Well-Being Index (US): life evaluation (Cantril ladder) rises steadily with log income; emotional well-being also rises but shows no further progress beyond about $75,000 a year.'
    - id: k2021
      title: Experienced well-being rises with income, even above $75,000 per year
      authors: Matthew A. Killingsworth
      year: 2021
      doi: 10.1073/pnas.2016976118
      finding: 'PNAS 118(4). 1,725,994 experience-sampling reports from 33,391 employed US adults: experienced and evaluative well-being both rise linearly with log(income), with no plateau above $75,000.'
    - id: kkm2023
      title: 'Income and emotional well-being: A conflict resolved'
      authors: Matthew A. Killingsworth, Daniel Kahneman and Barbara Mellers
      year: 2023
      doi: 10.1073/pnas.2208661120
      finding: 'PNAS 120(10). An adversarial collaboration: the flattening pattern exists only for the least happy 20% (happiness levels off above about $100,000); for happier people it rises steadily with log(income), and it accelerates in the happiest group.'

    text ev "Evaluation:\nthe ladder" #3d3833 x 850 y 560 font_size 32 hidden true
    text ex "Experience:\njoy, stress,\nsadness" #2f6b8f x 850 y 400 font_size 32 hidden true
    text kd "Kahneman & Deaton\n2010: 450,000\nanswers" #b5482e cites ["kd2010"] x 850 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    text p75 "≈ $75k" #b5482e cites ["kd2010"] x 850 y 330 font_size 34 hidden true
    text kil "Killingsworth 2021:\n1.7 million reports,\n33,000 adults" #2f6b8f cites ["k2021"] x 850 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    text adv "Adversarial\ncollaboration,\n2023" #3d3833 cites ["kkm2023"] x 850 y 560 font_size 30 hidden true
    text p100 "flat above\n≈ $100k" #b5482e cites ["kkm2023"] x 850 y 360 font_size 32 hidden true
    text schem "shapes only" #8f887c x 850 y 180 font_size 22 hidden true

    card title "Satisfied, or happy?"

A straight band, then, for the ladder. But the ladder asks you to judge your life, not how you felt today.
    draw axes line_1

B: Psychologists keep the two apart. Life e (@draw ev@)valuation is the ladder. Experienced wel (@draw ex@)l-being is the joy, stress and sadness of the moment.

Do they move together as income rises?
    highlight axes

    erase ev ex

B: In 2010 Daniel Kahneman and Angus Deaton looked at over four hundred and fifty thousand answers from Americans. (@highlight line_1@) Judging their lives, people rated them higher at every step up in income.
    draw kd schem

B: But yesterday's feelings stopped getting better at around seventy-five thousand dollars a year.
    draw line_2 p75

Money buys satisfaction, but not happiness, past that point. That's a famous headline.
    highlight line_2

    erase kd p75

B: And then it was challenged. In 2021 Matthew Killingsworth used 1.7 million moment-by-moment reports from 33,000 working Americans: feelings kept rising, with no plateau.
    draw kil line_3

    erase kil

Two careful studies, opposite answers. So the rivals teamed up, with Barbara Mellers as go-betwee (@draw adv@)n: an adversarial collaboration.
    highlight line_2 line_3

    erase line_1 line_2 line_3

B: Reanalysing the data in 2023, they found the plateau, but only for the least happy fifth of people, above about a hundred thousand dollars.
    draw line_4 p100

For everyone else, feelings keep rising with income, and for t (@draw line_6@)he happiest they rise even faster.
    draw line_5

So both were partly right. More money means better days for most people; above a poin (@highlight line_4@)t, it no longer eases the unhappiness of the unhappiest.
    highlight line_5 line_6

    pause 0.5

## Is it what we maximise?
sources:
    - id: easterlin2010
      title: The happiness–income paradox revisited
      authors: Richard A. Easterlin, Laura Angelescu McVey, Malgorzata Switek, Onnicha Sawangfa and Jacqueline Smith Zweig
      year: 2010
      doi: 10.1073/pnas.1015962107
      finding: PNAS 107(52). Over ten years or more, happiness does not rise with a country's income, in developed, developing and formerly socialist countries; in the short run happiness falls in contractions and rises in expansions.
    - id: sw2008
      title: 'Economic Growth and Subjective Well-Being: Reassessing the Easterlin Paradox'
      authors: Betsey Stevenson and Justin Wolfers
      year: 2008
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w14282
      finding: Brookings Papers on Economic Activity; in time series, economic growth is associated with rising happiness.
    - id: bhkr2012
      title: What Do You Think Would Make You Happier? What Do You Think You Would Choose?
      authors: Daniel J. Benjamin, Ori Heffetz, Miles S. Kimball and Alex Rees-Jones
      year: 2012
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w16489
      finding: American Economic Review 102(5). Choices and predicted happiness usually coincide, with systematic reversals; predicted sense of purpose, control over one's life, family happiness and social status help explain choice beyond predicted happiness.

    text h_left "A country over decades" #3d3833 x 270 y 630 font_size 32 hidden true
    path ax thick #3d3833 points [[80,600],[80,320],[470,320]] hidden true
    text t_time "time →" #8f887c x 420 y 295 font_size 22 hidden true
    path inc smooth #2f6b8f points [[100,340],[190,380],[280,440],[370,510],[450,580]] style.stroke_width 5 hidden true
    text inc_t "income" #2f6b8f x 385 y 585 font_size 28 hidden true
    path hap smooth #b5482e cites ["easterlin2010"] points [[100,450],[150,470],[200,430],[250,462],[300,425],[350,460],[400,432],[450,452]] style.stroke_width 5 hidden true
    text hap_t "happiness" #b5482e cites ["easterlin2010"] x 300 y 395 font_size 28 hidden true
    text illus "illustrative" #8f887c x 150 y 295 font_size 20 hidden true
    text why1 "compare with neighbours" #3d3833 x 275 y 240 font_size 28 hidden true
    text why2 "get used to it" #3d3833 x 275 y 195 font_size 28 hidden true
    text swd "Stevenson & Wolfers:\nrising together" #2f6b8f cites ["sw2008"] x 740 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    text h_right "What is U?" #3d3833 x 740 y 630 font_size 36 hidden true
    text u1 "a ranking of\nyour choices" #8a5fa8 x 740 y 535 font_size 32 hidden true
    text u2 "choice ≈ happiness…\nusually" #3d3833 cites ["bhkr2012"] x 740 y 420 font_size 30 hidden true
    text u3 "+ purpose, control,\nfamily, status" #5e8c4f cites ["bhkr2012"] x 740 y 310 font_size 30 hidden true
    text s1 "Richer → more satisfied: consensus" #5e8c4f x 500 y 540 font_size 40 hidden true
    text s2 "Growth → happier nation: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 420 font_size 40 hidden true
    text s3 "Happiness is all of U: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 300 font_size 40 hidden true
    text hb "Boatyard: only welders\nget +20%" #3d3833 x 500 y 420 font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "Is it what we maximise?"

    draw h_left ax t_time illus

Across people and across countries, then, more income goes with more satisfaction. Easterlin's reply: that's a snapshot.
    draw inc inc_t

B: In 2010 he and colleagues followed rich, poor and for (@draw hap hap_t@)merly communist countries for ten years or more. Happiness rose in booms and fe (@highlight hap@)ll in slumps, but did not climb with growth.

Why not? If what counts is your income com (@draw why1@)pared with your neighbours', everyone getting richer lifts no one. And  (@draw why2@)we get used to what we have.

B: Stevenson and Wolfers read the time series differently, and found growth and happ (@highlight hap@)iness rising together. That part is still argued over.
    draw swd

    erase swd

Which leaves the title question. To an (@draw h_right u1@) economist, U is just a score that ranks the choices you'd make, not a feeling you report.

B: Asked both what they'd choose and wh (@draw u2@)at would make them happier, people usually gave the same answer, but not always. Purpose, c (@draw u3@)ontrol over their lives, family and status pulled too.

    erase h_left ax t_time inc inc_t hap hap_t illus why1 why2 h_right u1 u2 u3

How sure are we? That richer people and (@draw s1@) richer countries report more life satisfaction: consensus.

That growth makes (@draw s2@) a nation happier over decades: contested. That happiness is all we maximi (@draw s3@)se: contested too, and the evidence so far says it's one aim among several.

    pause 0.5

    quiz "A $500 transfer to a family on $1,000 a year, or a $5,000 raise for a family on $100,000. On the cross-country slope, which moves the ladder more?" voice b
        right: The $500 transfer: it lifts that family's income by half, against five percent for the other.
        wrong: Count proportions, not dollars: $500 is half of $1,000, while $5,000 is a twentieth of $100,000. Here, the $500 transfer.
        + The $500 transfer
        * The $5,000 raise
        * About the same

    erase s1 s2 s3

    cards sortq check each
        Life evaluation: Where on the ladder are you?
        Feelings: Did you laugh a lot yesterday?
        Life evaluation: How satisfied are you with your life?
        Feelings: Did you feel stress yesterday?, How do you feel right now?

    ask "Which kind of well-being does each survey question measure? Sort the cards." on sortq store srt stage own
        right: All {srt.total} sorted: the ladder and satisfaction judge a life; laughter, stress and right now are feelings.
        wrong: You got {srt} of {srt.total}. Judging your life as a whole is evaluation; how you felt at a moment is feelings. Here: the ladder and satisfaction are evaluation; laughter, stress and right now are feelings.

    erase sortq

    draw hb

    quiz "The boatyard gives only its welders a 20% raise; prices are unchanged. If pay compared with co-workers is what matters, the painters' satisfaction…" voice b
        right: Falls: their own pay is unchanged, but their place against the welders drops.
        wrong: The painters' pay didn't move, but the welders' did, so their place in the yard slipped. Here, it falls.
        + falls
        * stays the same
        * rises

    erase hb

So happiness isn't the whole of what we maximise, but it is a yardstick we can hold our choices against. Across people and countries, money buys it by doubling (@highlight s1 s2@)s; whether growth buys a nation more is still argued.
    draw s1 s2 s3 parallel true

    pause 0.5

## Where next
