  lines:
    '|a||Is happiness what we maximise? And if it is, does more money buy more of it?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5680
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||Start with how anyone measures it. Gallup asks people in about a hundred and forty countries to picture a ladder: zero is the worst possible life for you, ten the best.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11280
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:chkd5m'
    '|a||Which step are you on? Average the answers and each country gets a score: its life satisfaction.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7240
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1ntn69d'
    '|a||Now set it against income: GDP per person, in dollars adjusted for what things cost locally.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6560
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1umtalj'
    '|b||My guess: it levels off. Once you''re comfortable, money stops buying happiness.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5040
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1rpmz75'
    '|a||Among the richest countries, does more income still go with higher life satisfaction?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6120
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1q4ga9c'
    '|a||Hold that guess: here come the data.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2880
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|a||Here are the countries, in 2025.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3120
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|a||Among the poorest the scores climb steeply. Among the rich, the cloud looks nearly flat.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6800
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1lcgjev'
    '|b||So I was right.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 1160
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:jiasbt'
    '|a||That''s close to what Richard Easterlin argued in 1974. Within a country, richer people do say they are happier.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7640
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:rrfi82'
    '|a||But as America grew richer from 1946 to 1970, its average happiness did not rise.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7120
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:u1ttsj'
    '|a||That''s the Easterlin paradox: richer beats poorer at any moment, yet growth doesn''t seem to lift a nation''s happiness over time.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8600
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1np22zc'
    '|b||The dollar scale is the wrong ruler. A thousand dollars more is a lot in Malawi and next to nothing in Norway.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7920
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|b||So count income in doublings instead: one thousand, two, four, eight, up to a hundred and twenty-eight thousand.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6840
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1jbpcz'
    '|a||Same countries, same scores. And the flat top is gone: it''s a straight, rising band.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6720
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1xmlaxy'
    '|b||Each doubling of income goes with about half a step up the ladder, from the poorest country to the richest.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6840
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:2wrh3l'
    '|a||That''s what Betsey Stevenson and Justin Wolfers showed in 2008: no point where it flattens, and much the same slope as between richer and poorer people inside one country.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9880
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1h4biax'
    '|b||So I wasn''t right after all: the flat top was the dollar scale talking. Forty to eighty thousand takes forty thousand dollars for the same half step that a thousand buys at the bottom.':
